What is cgrun
=============

cgrun is a Run box: a small window with a line to type in, a Run
button and a Close button. What is typed is run, as the program of
that name if the desktop knows one and as a command line if not, and
the window is gone. It is written for CGTK and comes from nowhere
else.

The line finishes itself as it is typed, from the lines that ran
before and then from the names of the programs that have a .desktop
file; what it adds is selected, so typing on replaces it. The lines
that ran before can be let down from the arrow at its end. A box
beneath runs the thing in a terminal.

A third button, Programs, opens a list of the programs and the earlier
command lines, which what is typed then narrows, with the best met and
the most used first. cgrun --list starts with the list open.

It is meant for a key. In Hyprland, for one:

  bind = SUPER, R, exec, cgrun

It has no .desktop file of its own and is in no menu. Its window
cannot be resized, which tiling compositors take as a reason to float
it; its class and application id is Cgrun. There is only ever one: a
cgrun started while another is there takes its place.

It is an ordinary window, and has the keyboard only as long as the
window manager leaves it there. Where the keyboard follows the pointer
a rule can keep it, as in Hyprland 0.54:

  windowrule = match:class ^(Cgrun)$, stay_focused on

It reads the .desktop files afresh each time it starts, and keeps no
process running in between. What it remembers, how often each program
was started and the last fifty command lines, is in
$XDG_STATE_HOME/cgrun/history.


Building
========

See the README at the top of cgtools. cgrun needs a CGTK whose GLib
has key files and g_spawn_async(); configure looks for them.


Use
===

  cgrun [--list]

  Return            run the line; with the list open, start what is
                    chosen in it, or the line if the list is empty
  Control+Return    run the line as a command line, in a terminal
  Shift+Return      with the list open, run the line whatever the
                    list has
  Up, Down          go through the lines that ran before; with the
                    list open, choose in it
  Escape            go

The man page, cgrun(1), has the rest.
